        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "Last month, nearly a dozen wildfires erupted across southern California, sending plumes of smoke and particulate matter into the air. Public health officials in Los Angeles issued a multiday air quality advisory for the county, warning of â€œpotential direct smoke impactâ€ and advising everyone who could see or smell smoke to â€œavoid unnecessary outdoor exposure and to limit physical exertion.â€ The red zone on the map included Los Angeles Stadium â€”&nbsp;also known as SoFi Stadium â€” one of the venues for the World Cup, soccerâ€™s marquee event, which begins on Thursday. Between June 12 and July 10, Los Angeles will host eight games and is expected to draw tens of thousands of fans and scores of players.&nbsp; As dry, hot, conditions persist, more fires are possible and smoke could once again loom over the stadium. The same risk exists for a number of the 15 other World Cup host cities. But, despite the documented health impacts of smoke exposure, FIFA, the international governing body for soccer, doesnâ€™t appear to have a plan if the air quality deteriorates.&nbsp; â€œFIFA has basically almost done nothing,â€ said Nicholas Watanabe, a professor of sport and entertainment management at the University of South Carolina. â€œThey are lagging behind even minor leagues in North America.â€ The National Womenâ€™s Soccer League , or NWSL; the Canadian Football League ; and the NCAA , which oversees college sports, all have at least some guidelines outlining what to do if the Air Quality Index reaches certain thresholds. Other leagues â€” from Major League Baseball to the Womenâ€™s National Basketball Association â€” have postponed games because of wildfire smoke , notably when plumes spread across Canada and North America in Juƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;AhqEUb4nZ51lXQmMxi4plew7EASUMrNiGrberxR61pAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcurrentƒdpairƒhconstant‚db